## Phase 5 — Automated Evaluation & Ablation Benchmark
**Why:** This gives you the real measured numbers to replace the unverified "35% recall improvement" on your resume. This is the phase that makes interviewers say "wow."

### Task 5.1: Expand `generate_test_pdfs.py` into a golden dataset
- **Modify:** [generate_test_pdfs.py](file:///c:/Users/deepa/OneDrive/Desktop/projects/Contract_Checker-main/Contract_Checker-main/generate_test_pdfs.py)
- **Current:** Creates 4 policy rules + 5 contract clauses with labeled edge cases
- **Expand to:** 25-30 test cases covering:
  - ✅ Exact compliance matches
  - ❌ Subtle numeric deviations (Net-30 vs policy Net-45)
  - 🔗 Cross-referenced clauses ("as defined in Section 2.1")
  - 🚫 Omitted mandatory clauses (policy requires it, contract skips it)
  - 🤷 Out-of-scope boilerplate (no policy applies → should be marked as such)
  - 🔄 Paraphrased/synonym clauses (vocabulary mismatch test)
- **Output:** `eval/golden_dataset.json` with ground-truth labels:
```json
{
  "clause_text": "Vendor shall notify Customer of security incidents within 14 days",
  "expected_policy_section": "Section 4.2",
  "expected_status": "NON_COMPLIANT",
  "expected_reason": "Policy requires 24-hour notification, contract says 14 days"
}
```

### Task 5.2: Build `eval/evaluate.py` — the automated benchmark runner
- **New file:** `eval/evaluate.py`
- **Metrics computed:**

| Metric | What It Measures | What Decision It Drives |
|---|---|---|
| **Recall@3** | Does the correct policy rule appear in top 3 retrieved chunks? | Whether we need more retrieval candidates |
| **MRR@3** | How close to #1 is the correct policy rule? | Whether reranking is effective |
| **Verdict F1** | Accuracy of COMPLIANT/NON_COMPLIANT classification | Whether prompts/LLM need improvement |
| **Citation Faithfulness %** | % of quotes that pass substring verification | Whether hallucination prevention works |
| **Missing Clause Detection Rate** | % of omitted mandatory clauses caught | Whether bidirectional check works |
| **End-to-End Latency (p50/p95)** | Wall-clock seconds per contract | Whether batching/caching are effective |
| **Total API Calls** | Count of LLM + embedding + reranker calls | Whether efficiency upgrades work |
| **Token Cost ($)** | Input + output tokens at provider rates | ROI of micro-batching and caching |

### Task 5.3: Run 4-config ablation study
- Run the same 25-30 test cases across 4 retrieval configurations:

| Config | Description | What It Proves |
|---|---|---|
| A | FAISS only (dense search, no BM25) | Baseline — where semantic search alone fails |
| B | BM25 only (keyword search, no FAISS) | Baseline — where keyword search alone fails |
| C | Naive Hybrid (BM25 + FAISS, fixed 0.5/0.5, no reranker) | Shows hybrid is better than either alone |
| D | **Full pipeline** (Contextual chunks + Weighted RRF + Reranker + Score Gate) | Proves every component adds value |

- **Output:** A markdown table + bar chart showing Recall@3, MRR@3, and Latency for each config
- **Resume line becomes:** "Validated via automated ablation benchmark: contextual hybrid retrieval with cross-encoder reranking improved Recall@3 from X% to Y% over dense-only baselines"

**Acceptance criteria for Phase 5:**
- [ ] Golden dataset with 25+ labeled test cases
- [ ] `python -m eval.evaluate` runs end-to-end and outputs all metrics
- [ ] Ablation table shows measurable improvement per component
- [ ] Real numbers replace the unverified "35% recall" claim

